# ============ 英文摘要 ============
{"t": "pagebreak"},
{"t": "en_title", "x": "The Pollution- and Carbon-Reduction Effects of National 5A Scenic Area Construction and Their Mechanisms"},
{"t": "en_sub", "x": "Evidence from a Staggered Difference-in-Differences Analysis of Chinese Prefecture-Level Cities"},
{"t": "abs_h", "x": "Abstract"},
{"t": "abs_en", "x": "Under the dual constraints of China’s carbon peaking and carbon neutrality goals and the ongoing battle against pollution, how non-command-and-control institutional arrangements can promote the synergistic reduction of pollutant and carbon emissions has become a critical question. This paper treats the designation of National 5A Tourist Attractions as a quality-certification institution that combines brand-based incentives with dynamic review and delisting constraints. Using panel data on Chinese prefecture-level and above cities from 2003 to 2024 and a staggered difference-in-differences design, we find that 5A designation significantly reduces the synergistic pollution-carbon emission level of host cities by about 10 percent. The result is robust to parallel-trend and event-study tests, heterogeneity-robust estimators, synthetic difference-in-differences, double machine learning, placebo tests, propensity score matching, instrumental variable estimation, and a battery of variable-replacement and sample-adjustment checks. Mechanism tests indicate that the policy works through three channels: dual governance drivers (stronger environmental regulation and heightened public environmental concern), structural and technological transformation (industrial servitization and green technology innovation), and energy efficiency improvement (lower energy intensity). Heterogeneity analysis shows stronger effects in non-resource-based cities, central cities, cities with multiple 5A attractions, and cities with nature-based attractions. Spatial econometric results reveal that while local emission performance improves, positive spillovers to neighboring cities turn the regional total effect positive, suggesting a risk of nearby transfer of high-emission activities. Decoupling tests further show that the policy significantly promotes the relative decoupling of economic growth from environmental pressure."},
{"t": "abs_kw_en", "x": "Key Words: National 5A Tourist Attraction; Synergistic Reduction of Pollution and Carbon Emissions; Staggered Difference-in-Differences; Spatial Spillover; Green Transformation"},
{"t": "abs_kw_en", "x": "JEL Classification: Q56; R11; L83"},
